{"A":"Own a conservative serial fast-path. Use only isomorphism invariants derived from the populated adjacency matrix (including diagonal/self-loops): size, row-popcount/loop signatures, histograms, and joint refinement. Reject only with a proof; otherwise retain faithful enumeration. Evaluate packed rows/cached signatures versus vector<bool>, but preserve every matching permutation in the original Heap visitation order and formatting.","B":"Own an exact decision/uniqueness solver track. Use refined candidate domains and incremental checks of both edges and nonedges, including loops. A safe hybrid is: zero mappings => non-isomorphic message; exactly one proven mapping => print its single reference-format line; two or more => fall back to the legacy Heap enumeration so multi-solution order remains exact. Never substitute an arbitrary first witness.","C":"Own the difficult multi-solution and parallel research track. Investigate constraint-guided enumeration only if mappings can be emitted in the exact original Heap order; arbitrary DFS/next_permutation order is unacceptable. If parallelizing on four ARM cores, buffer and merge by Heap visit order, use a size/work threshold, and keep output serial. The 8.49 ms baseline makes thread startup and buffering likely risks.","G":"Integrate only measured, output-identical candidates; gains are not additive. Start with A’s safe rejection/layout work, add B conditionally before legacy enumeration, and accept C only if its ordered multi-solution path wins. Preserve parser behavior, self-loops, trailing-space/newline output, identity behavior, and all-match behavior. Validate against the reference across non-isomorphic, rigid isomorphic, and symmetric graphs; do not tune to cases or alter the harness.","analysis":"The only source is factorial graph-isomorphism enumeration; baseline is valid at 8.487 ms on 4-core ARM64, so fixed overhead matters. Observable source behavior is stricter than a one-witness solver: it prints identity immediately when valid, otherwise prints every valid mapping encountered by Heap’s algorithm, and accepts diagonal edges. Prioritize provable rejection and uniqueness detection; retain the legacy path whenever ordering cannot be reproduced."}